From: Codex folio backport <codex@local>
Subject: [PATCH] E404 folio G2.5b: folio_add_lru() and filemap_add_folio()

Upstream patches 83/90 (mm/swap: Add folio_add_lru) and 86/90 (mm/filemap:
Add filemap_add_folio).  They go together because 86 calls folio_add_lru().

__add_to_page_cache_locked() becomes __filemap_add_folio(), and
add_to_page_cache_lru() becomes filemap_add_folio().  Both old entry points
survive as thin wrappers so the seven in-tree callers (three in mm/filemap.c,
four in fs/cachefiles/rdwr.c) keep compiling unchanged.

Divergences from upstream, all deliberate:

  - __filemap_add_folio() stays static.  Upstream exports it and drops the
    ALLOW_ERROR_INJECTION() and the BTF_ID() in kernel/bpf/verifier.c only
    because error injection and BTF need the symbol to be visible.  The E404
    has neither on this function, so exporting it would be noise.

  - The memcg charge keeps E404's three-phase protocol, taking the head page:
    mem_cgroup_try_charge() -> mem_cgroup_commit_charge(), or
    mem_cgroup_cancel_charge() on the error path.  Upstream calls
    mem_cgroup_charge() and mem_cgroup_uncharge() here, neither of which
    exists in the E404 4.19 base -- that is the memcg_data/obj_cgroup rework
    from upstream patches 38-43, which is not portable to this tree.

  - __inc_node_page_state() is kept, not __lruvec_stat_add_folio().  E404
    accounts NR_FILE_PAGES per node, and the lruvec-stat helpers come from
    the vmstat rework in patches 50-56.

  - The xarray split hunks are dropped: they need xa_get_order(),
    xas_split_alloc() and xas_split(), none of which are in E404.  They came
    with the multi-page xarray support that is part of the same
    memcg_data/obj_cgroup rework.

  - The xarray still stores a page pointer, so xas_store() takes &folio->page
    rather than the folio as upstream does.  The addresses are identical, but
    naming the page is honest about what the xarray holds in this tree.

  - folio_add_lru() calls MGLRU's __lru_cache_add(&folio->page) instead of
    inlining the body with pagevec_add_and_need_flush(), which does not exist
    in E404.  MGLRU already split lru_cache_add() into exactly this shape,
    so the folio version reuses that body verbatim.

  - The compatibility wrappers stay inline in mm/filemap.c, mm/swap.c and
    include/linux/pagemap.h rather than moving to mm/folio-compat.c, which
    this tree does not have.  That is the same choice made for
    add_page_wait_queue() in folio G2.3f.

  - xas_store() aside, the new natural-alignment assertion is added as
    upstream writes it.  At order 0, which is every folio in this
    configuration, folio_nr_pages() is 1, so index & (1 - 1) is 0 and it can
    never fire; it becomes live only if some caller passes order > 1.

diff --git a/include/linux/pagemap.h b/include/linux/pagemap.h
index 3b62d17..89aafaa 100644
--- a/include/linux/pagemap.h
+++ b/include/linux/pagemap.h
@@ -854,8 +854,18 @@ static inline int fault_in_pages_readable(const char __user *uaddr, int size)
 
 int add_to_page_cache_locked(struct page *page, struct address_space *mapping,
 				pgoff_t index, gfp_t gfp_mask);
-int add_to_page_cache_lru(struct page *page, struct address_space *mapping,
-				pgoff_t index, gfp_t gfp_mask);
+int filemap_add_folio(struct address_space *mapping, struct folio *folio,
+			pgoff_t index, gfp_t gfp);
+
+/*
+ * Compatibility wrapper for callers not yet converted to folios.  Upstream
+ * puts this in mm/folio-compat.c, which this tree does not have.
+ */
+static inline int add_to_page_cache_lru(struct page *page,
+		struct address_space *mapping, pgoff_t index, gfp_t gfp_mask)
+{
+	return filemap_add_folio(mapping, page_folio(page), index, gfp_mask);
+}
 extern void delete_from_page_cache(struct page *page);
 extern void __delete_from_page_cache(struct page *page, void *shadow);
 int replace_page_cache_page(struct page *old, struct page *new, gfp_t gfp_mask);
